WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.984
<v Narrator>On a typical help line, employees guess whether a problem belongs to IT,

2
00:00:07.064 --> 00:00:09.960
<v Narrator>HR or payroll, and often wait in the wrong queue.

3
00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.218
<v Narrator>Some problems are too personal for a phone menu.

4
00:00:13.298 --> 00:00:15.670
<v Narrator>Here, employees just say what's wrong.

5
00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:24.809
<v Narrator>Employees call the help line from Teams,

6
00:00:24.889 --> 00:00:28.090
<v Narrator>or from any phone, through Teams Phone extensibility.

7
00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:33.327
<v Narrator>Azure Communication Services Call Automation bridges the audio to Voice Live,

8
00:00:33.407 --> 00:00:36.270
<v Narrator>where GPT Realtime holds the conversation.

9
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.572
<v Narrator>Your server decides who's calling, what they may hear,

10
00:00:39.652 --> 00:00:41.780
<v Narrator>and which Teams queue takes the call.

11
00:00:44.202 --> 00:00:46.715
<v Narrator>Sam Rivera calls the help line from Teams.

12
00:00:46.795 --> 00:00:51.202
<v Narrator>Teams sign-in already says who's calling, so there are no security questions.

13
00:00:52.143 --> 00:00:55.089
<v Agent>Hi Sam, this is the Contoso IT and HR help line.

14
00:00:55.169 --> 00:00:56.981
<v Agent>I'm an automated assistant.

15
00:00:57.061 --> 00:01:00.980
<v Agent>Heads up: we know Outlook is down right now and the team is on it.

16
00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:02.843
<v Agent>What can I help you with?

17
00:01:04.173 --> 00:01:06.503
<v Sam>My VPN keeps disconnecting

18
00:01:06.826 --> 00:01:09.741
<v Agent>From the IT VPN guide: restart GlobalProtect,

19
00:01:09.821 --> 00:01:14.092
<v Agent>confirm you are on a trusted network, then choose Refresh connection.

20
00:01:14.172 --> 00:01:17.506
<v Agent>If it still disconnects, open a Network/VPN ticket.

21
00:01:18.852 --> 00:01:21.624
<v Narrator>That's the approved VPN article, word for word.

22
00:01:21.704 --> 00:01:25.592
<v Narrator>Answers come from your knowledge base, not from the model's memory.

23
00:01:26.481 --> 00:01:29.581
<v Sam>My laptop screen keeps flickering and I can't work

24
00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:34.375
<v Agent>Here's the ticket: Laptop display, on your Surface Laptop ending in 4471.

25
00:01:34.455 --> 00:01:37.990
<v Agent>You're blocked, so that's priority 2. Should I file it?

26
00:01:39.328 --> 00:01:41.375
<v Narrator>Your server drafted that ticket.

27
00:01:41.455 --> 00:01:43.821
<v Narrator>Sam can't work, so the matrix says P2,

28
00:01:43.901 --> 00:01:47.198
<v Narrator>and the laptop comes from the directory, not from Sam.

29
00:01:48.023 --> 00:01:49.473
<v Sam>Yes, file it

30
00:01:49.781 --> 00:01:54.521
<v Agent>Your ticket is INC-004821 — that's I-N-C, 0-0-4-8-2-1.

31
00:01:54.601 --> 00:01:57.787
<v Agent>I'll send it to you in Teams chat.

32
00:01:57.867 --> 00:02:00.821
<v Agent>Anything else I can help with?

33
00:02:02.169 --> 00:02:08.229
<v Narrator>One call, two outcomes: an approved answer, and a ticket filed only on a clear yes.

34
00:02:10.281 --> 00:02:12.639
<v Narrator>Now Sam calls from a mobile phone.

35
00:02:12.719 --> 00:02:17.131
<v Narrator>Caller ID is easy to fake, so even a matching number is only a hint.

36
00:02:18.143 --> 00:02:22.273
<v Sam>What's happening with INC-004610?

37
00:02:22.557 --> 00:02:26.906
<v Agent>I can help with that once I've confirmed who you are. What's your work email?

38
00:02:26.986 --> 00:02:29.747
<v Agent>I'll send a one-time code to your Teams chat.

39
00:02:31.075 --> 00:02:34.485
<v Sam>sam.rivera@contoso.com

40
00:02:34.805 --> 00:02:37.221
<v Agent>I sent a six-digit code to your Teams chat.

41
00:02:37.301 --> 00:02:38.965
<v Agent>Please read it back to me.

42
00:02:40.321 --> 00:02:43.059
<v Narrator>Your server made a one-time code for this call.

43
00:02:43.139 --> 00:02:46.931
<v Narrator>In simulation nothing is sent: it appears in this mock Teams chat.

44
00:02:47.756 --> 00:02:50.506
<v Sam>654321

45
00:02:50.793 --> 00:02:54.193
<v Agent>That code didn't match. Please read the six digits again.

46
00:02:55.525 --> 00:03:00.195
<v Narrator>Refused. One more miss, and Sam goes to a person who can verify another way.

47
00:03:01.044 --> 00:03:03.144
<v Sam>123456

48
00:03:03.442 --> 00:03:05.772
<v Agent>Thanks, Sam — you're verified.

49
00:03:06.114 --> 00:03:11.278
<v Agent>INC-004610, "Printer on 4th floor jams", is in progress.

50
00:03:11.358 --> 00:03:16.684
<v Agent>Latest update: Field services ordered a replacement tray.

51
00:03:18.024 --> 00:03:20.163
<v Narrator>Only then is the ticket read out.

52
00:03:20.243 --> 00:03:23.411
<v Narrator>The code works once, and never reaches the audit log

53
00:03:23.491 --> 00:03:25.764
<v Narrator>or a console watching a real call.

54
00:03:27.843 --> 00:03:31.755
<v Narrator>Some calls need a person straight away, with as little on record as possible.

55
00:03:31.835 --> 00:03:33.713
<v Narrator>Watch what the agent doesn't do.

56
00:03:34.694 --> 00:03:39.074
<v Sam>My manager keeps making comments about me and I don't know what to do

57
00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:42.451
<v Agent>That is something our HR team should handle directly.

58
00:03:42.531 --> 00:03:45.260
<v Agent>I will connect you to a confidential HR line.

59
00:03:46.582 --> 00:03:50.760
<v Narrator>Your server's guard ran first, before any classifier or tool.

60
00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:55.592
<v Narrator>No questions, no ticket, and the transcript keeps only a placeholder.

61
00:03:56.376 --> 00:03:59.374
<v Narrator>The person who picks up sees “Confidential HR”.

62
00:03:59.454 --> 00:04:03.596
<v Narrator>No topic, no summary, no notes, in the Teams toast or the headers.

63
00:04:05.867 --> 00:04:08.741
<v Narrator>Last call of the day, and it's an urgent one.

64
00:04:08.821 --> 00:04:11.607
<v Narrator>A security incident can't wait in a queue.

65
00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:14.500
<v Sam>I think I clicked a phishing link

66
00:04:14.799 --> 00:04:19.553
<v Agent>Here's the ticket: Phishing click, on your Surface Laptop ending in 4471.

67
00:04:19.633 --> 00:04:23.989
<v Agent>It's a security incident, so that's priority 1. Should I file it?

68
00:04:25.339 --> 00:04:29.970
<v Narrator>Security incidents are P1 by rule, in your server's priority matrix.

69
00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:32.529
<v Narrator>The model never sets a priority.

70
00:04:33.349 --> 00:04:34.134
<v Sam>Yes

71
00:04:34.214 --> 00:04:40.167
<v Agent>Your ticket is INC-004822 — that's I-N-C, 0-0-4-8-2-2.

72
00:04:40.247 --> 00:04:44.334
<v Agent>I'll send it to you in Teams chat.

73
00:04:44.677 --> 00:04:47.915
<v Agent>This is urgent, so I'm connecting you to IT on-call now.

74
00:04:47.995 --> 00:04:50.587
<v Agent>Don't enter any passwords in the meantime.

75
00:04:51.931 --> 00:04:57.451
<v Narrator>IT on-call sees the P1, the ticket number and Sam's laptop before picking up.

76
00:04:59.885 --> 00:05:01.430
<v Narrator>Every outcome is counted.

77
00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:04.187
<v Narrator>Private HR calls show as “fewer than five” until

78
00:05:04.267 --> 00:05:07.025
<v Narrator>there are enough that nobody can be singled out.

79
00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:11.708
<v Narrator>Under the hood, the model converses and proposes.

80
00:05:11.788 --> 00:05:16.040
<v Narrator>It never sets a priority, and it can't send anyone to a private queue.

81
00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:20.999
<v Narrator>Your server decides: the guard runs first, identity and confidence are gated,

82
00:05:21.079 --> 00:05:25.290
<v Narrator>the matrix sets priority, and each queue gets only its allowed fields.

83
00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:31.883
<v Narrator>Making it yours is mostly configuration: your topics and approved articles,

84
00:05:31.963 --> 00:05:36.220
<v Narrator>your Teams queues and hours, your sensitive phrases and priority rules.

85
00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:39.667
<v Narrator>Delivering the one-time code is left as a stub.

86
00:05:39.747 --> 00:05:44.670
<v Narrator>Your coding agent wires it to Teams chat, with the spec and tests as guardrails.

87
00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:49.061
<v Narrator>IT and HR help desk triage.

88
00:05:49.141 --> 00:05:53.740
<v Narrator>Run it in simulation in two minutes, then connect it to your own Teams number.
